{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB12141 - MONDO_18256",
  "verbalization": "Gilteritinib is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) with the T(8;16)(p11;p13) translocation, as evidenced by its association with both Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia With Maturation and Acute Myeloblastic Leukemia Without Maturation, both of which can progress to AML with the specified translocation. The shared indication for Quizartinib, which is a known inhibitor of the tyrosine kinase domain of the AML1-ETO protein, suggests a potential mechanism of action involving the targeting of the AML1-ETO fusion protein. Gilteritinib’s ability to interact with the AML1-ETO protein, which is central to the pathogenesis of this leukemia, may underlie its therapeutic potential. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Gilteritinib may be a viable candidate for repurposing in AML with the T(8;16)(p11;p13) translocation, particularly in cases where the disease is resistant to other therapies. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of targeting the AML1-ETO fusion protein, a key driver of AML progression, and suggests that Gilteritinib could offer a novel therapeutic approach for this subset of patients.",
